Cell["\<\
Comparing coefficients at both basis we see that they are exactly the same.  \
Reciprocal basis elements can be conveniently input by first entering an \
empty list and then a list with up indices, for example, typing   \
\[DoubleStruckE][{},{1,3}] in the input cell gives a basis bivector ( in x-z \
plane). Typing indices in both list, you will get mixed basis. In order to \
perform computations, however, you often will need to rise or lower all \
indices. That is implementation limitation, since we don\[CloseCurlyQuote]t \
want to dealt with so many different possibilities. In simple cases, however, \
this works without additional attempts\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"52920b7a-d42f-4a74-b5aa-7f864cb00d6d"],

