Cell["\<\
9-th, 10-th and 11-th  formulas, nothing to test, since they simply expands \
linear function into primary  basis or define adjoint function\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"31c733e3-dc7a-45dd-befe-aabb5458b599"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "12-th formula, ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   TemplateBox[<|"boxes" -> FormBox[
       RowBox[{"\[Del]", "\[Congruent]", 
         SubscriptBox["\[Del]", 
          StyleBox["x", "TI"]], "\[LongEqual]", 
         SuperscriptBox[
          StyleBox["e", FontSlant -> "Plain"], 
          StyleBox["i", "TI"]], 
         FractionBox["\[PartialD]", 
          RowBox[{"\[PartialD]", 
            SuperscriptBox[
             StyleBox["x", "TI"], 
             StyleBox["i", "TI"]]}]], "\[Congruent]", 
         SuperscriptBox[
          StyleBox["e", FontSlant -> "Plain"], 
          StyleBox["i", "TI"]], 
         SubscriptBox["\[PartialD]", 
          StyleBox["i", "TI"]]}], TraditionalForm], "errors" -> {}, "input" -> 
     "\\nabla\\equiv\\nabla_x=\\mathrm{e}_{}^i\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial\n\
x^i}\\equiv\\mathrm{e}_{}^i\\partial_{i}", "state" -> "Boxes"|>,
    "TeXAssistantTemplate"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "9f206b73-9616-45a9-bf0c-b0517f605397"],
 "  is a definition of vector derivative operator to be covered in details in \
calculus part. Explanation will be postponed. We only mention that to perform \
differentiation we have to define coordinate system (here \
\[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]Euclidean3DCS\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote]). To define \
coordinate system we first  define position vector (here in Euclidean 3D \
space). Then coordinate system parameters and other needed quantities  are \
computed."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"2fae0a04-ae11-46a0-987a-ef1ef27c7c73"],

