Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[TextData[{
 
 CounterBox["Section"],
 ".",
 
 CounterBox["Item"],
 "\t",
 StyleBox["Norm and semi-norm",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 ".  In the literature, a number of different definitions of norm (aka \
magnitude) are provided for seemingly the same purpose. Examples of norms are \
presented in the following units: vector norm, ",
 StyleBox["unit~\\ref{magnvector}",
  FontColor->RGBColor[1, 0, 0]],
 "; outer product norm,  ",
 StyleBox["unit~\\ref{outerproductvec}",
  FontColor->RGBColor[1, 0, 0]],
 "; bivector norm, ",
 StyleBox["unit~\\ref{magnbivector}",
  FontColor->RGBColor[1, 0, 0]],
 "; MV norm, ",
 StyleBox["unit~\\ref{MVmag}",
  FontColor->RGBColor[1, 0, 0]],
 "; Hermitian norm, ",
 StyleBox["unit~\\ref{idemp+mli}",
  FontColor->RGBColor[1, 0, 0]],
 "; real spinor norm, ",
 StyleBox["unit~\\ref{GArealIdempotentIdeal}",
  FontColor->RGBColor[1, 0, 0]],
 "; complex spinor norm, ",
 StyleBox["unit~\\ref{complexspinornorm}",
  FontColor->RGBColor[1, 0, 0]],
 "; ",
 StyleBox["paravector norm~\\ref{veccoord}",
  FontColor->RGBColor[1, 0, 0]],
 ". A more complex but more general norm is the determinant norm. It is \
related to inverse MV and described in the ",
 StyleBox["unit~\\ref{Detnorm}",
  FontColor->RGBColor[1, 0, 0]],
 ".\n\nIn algebras with mixed signature the concept of a semi-norm is \
introduced. For example, for MV ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   TemplateBox[<|"boxes" -> FormBox[
       RowBox[{
         StyleBox["B", "TI"], "\[LongEqual]", 
         SubscriptBox[
          StyleBox["b", "TI"], "0"], "+", 
         SubscriptBox[
          StyleBox["b", "TI"], "1"], 
         SubscriptBox[
          StyleBox["e", FontSlant -> "Plain"], "1"]}], TraditionalForm], 
     "errors" -> {}, "input" -> "\\m{B}=b_0+b_1\\e{1}", "state" -> "Boxes"|>,
    "TeXAssistantTemplate"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "655906ff-1e3c-44af-8fff-a09d893a5f2a"],
 " in hyperbolic number algebras ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   TemplateBox[<|"boxes" -> FormBox[
       SubscriptBox["Cl", 
        RowBox[{"1", ",", "0"}]], TraditionalForm], "errors" -> {}, "input" -> 
     "\\cl{1}{0}", "state" -> "Boxes"|>,
    "TeXAssistantTemplate"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "3304e9a0-9629-4150-a4a9-444c803974ee"],
 " one has ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   TemplateBox[<|"boxes" -> FormBox[
       RowBox[{
         StyleBox["B", "TI"], 
         OverscriptBox[
          StyleBox["B", "TI"], "\[Cap]", LimitsPositioning -> False], 
         "\[LongEqual]", 
         SubsuperscriptBox[
          StyleBox["b", "TI"], "0", "2"], "-", 
         SubsuperscriptBox[
          StyleBox["b", "TI"], "1", "2"]}], TraditionalForm], "errors" -> {}, 
     "input" -> "\\m{B}\\gradeinverse{\\m{B}}=b_0^2-b_1^2", "state" -> 
     "Boxes"|>,
    "TeXAssistantTemplate"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "22c40885-1c19-4ce6-b0ec-9d9d01815c34"],
 ", which may be positive, negative or zero. Therefore in this case a more \
general norm called a (determinant) semi-norm or pseudo-norm ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   TemplateBox[<|"boxes" -> FormBox[
       RowBox[{"||", 
         StyleBox["B", "TI"], "||", "\[LongEqual]", 
         SqrtBox[
          RowBox[{"abs", "(", 
            RowBox[{
              StyleBox["B", "TI"], 
              OverscriptBox[
               StyleBox["B", "TI"], "\[Cap]", LimitsPositioning -> False]}], 
            ")"}]], "\[LongEqual]", 
         SqrtBox[
          RowBox[{"abs", "(", 
            RowBox[{
              SubsuperscriptBox[
               StyleBox["b", "TI"], "0", "2"], "-", 
              SubsuperscriptBox[
               StyleBox["b", "TI"], "1", "2"]}], ")"}]], "\[GreaterEqual]", 
         "0"}], TraditionalForm], "errors" -> {}, "input" -> 
     "\\lVert\\m{B}\\rVert=\\sqrt{\\text{abs}(\\m{B}\\gradeinverse{\\m{B}})}=\
\\sqrt{\\text{abs}(b_0^2-b_1^2)}\\ge 0", "state" -> "Boxes"|>,
    "TeXAssistantTemplate"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "b0480bed-0f12-484d-b3e9-6b4332594245"],
 " is introduced. Note that the semi-norm ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   TemplateBox[<|"boxes" -> FormBox[
       RowBox[{"||", 
         StyleBox["B", "TI"], "||"}], TraditionalForm], "errors" -> {}, 
     "input" -> "\\lVert\\m{B}\\rVert", "state" -> "Boxes"|>,
    "TeXAssistantTemplate"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "c4ff2c6a-e22d-4ab7-86a4-ce0e67ff14ff"],
 " may be zero even if ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   TemplateBox[<|"boxes" -> FormBox[
       RowBox[{
         StyleBox["B", "TI"], "\[NotEqual]", "0"}], TraditionalForm], 
     "errors" -> {}, "input" -> "\\m{B}\\ne 0", "state" -> "Boxes"|>,
    "TeXAssistantTemplate"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "bc582a7a-e92e-446d-bec6-d8ccd50fe04d"],
 ". In case of the norm  the equality sign would require the MV to be equal \
to zero too. "
}], "Item",
 CellTags->
  "MagnitudeMain",ExpressionUUID->"5f1f7882-ec57-42a3-959f-8fcfc275af8c"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Computational verification", "ItemParagraph",
 FontWeight->"Bold",
 FontColor->RGBColor[
  0.6, 0.4, 0.2],ExpressionUUID->"e396d988-55a7-4e49-8099-da265cdb5cb6"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["\<\
Here we will introduced norms/magnitudes defined in the package.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"6918bed9-9037-4230-bb79-97d992f67bd0"],

Cell["\<\
There are many quite confusing norms defined for  general Clifford algebras. \
There is a list which is currently implemented
1)Norm of coefficients,  
2) Norm of coefficients normalized (when coefficient norm is not zero)
3)gaNorm2ReverseSigned, where MV is multiplied by reversed MV and scalar part \
is taken 
4) gaNormReverseAbs, which is obtained from 3 by taking absolute value and \
then square root 
5) gaNorm2CliffordConjugateSigned, similar to 3, except that reverse is \
replaced by Clifford conjugate
6) gaNormCliffordConjugateAbs,  obtained from 5 in the same way as 4 (from 3)
7) gaNormHermitian \
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.531215444705543*^9, 3.531215453183996*^9}, {
   3.531215728886285*^9, 3.531215752489262*^9}, {3.531215819529191*^9, 
